<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Hymn to the Aten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">middle of the 14th century BC</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> is the longest of several hymn-poems addressed to the Aten, the sun-disk worshipped as the supreme god under the 18th Dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>. It was composed in the middle of the 14th century BCE and is attributed, depending on the version, to Akhenaten himself or to his courtiers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The hymn belongs to the religious art of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> period, which produced literature, new temples and a whole new city, Akhet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, at the site of present-day Amarna, built as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> forbade the worship of other gods, a radical departure from centuries of Egyptian religious practice. His reforms were later regarded as heretical and reversed under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>, and some scholars have called them monotheistic, while others consider them henotheistic. Courtiers' rock tombs at Amarna carry similar prayers to the Aten or to the Aten and Akhenaten jointly. One, found in almost identical form in five tombs, is known as the Short Hymn to the Aten.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The long version was found in the tomb of the courtier, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, Ay, and its first part is damaged and unreadable. The surviving text addresses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> as the sole god, like whom there is no other, who created the world alone. It names the countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, says that the god settles every man in his place and supplies his needs, and notes that tongues, natures and skins differ among peoples.</p><p>In the last part, translated by Miriam Lichtheim, the speaker says that no one knows the god except his son Neferkheprure, Sole-one-of-Re, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, whom the god has taught his ways, and the text closes with praise of the king and of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>. The Egyptologist John Darnell asserts that the hymn was sung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The hymn resembles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a>. James Henry Breasted thought the psalm was inspired by it, and Arthur Weigall asked whether both derived from a common Syrian source or the psalm from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s poem. Lichtheim held that the likeness more probably reflects the generic similarity between Egyptian hymns and biblical psalms, and Mark S. Smith notes that enthusiasm for influence has cooled, although Jan Assmann and Donald Redford still argue for Egyptian influence.</p><p>Dominic Montserrat calls such texts formal eulogies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> and the royal couple rather than outpourings of emotion, and argues that they redefine the relation of god and king to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s advantage. Redford finds little evidence that Akhenaten originated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. Philip Glass set the hymn to music in his 1984 opera Akhnaten.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Hymn to the Aten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hymns to the gods were inscribed in temples and tombs; the <b>Great Hymn to the Aten</b>, from the time of Akhenaten, celebrates the sun as the creator of all life in terms often compared with Psalm 104 of the Hebrew Bible.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The New Kingdom added love poems, the Tale of the Two Brothers, the <b>Great Hymn to the Aten</b> and the poem on the battle of Kadesh of Ramesses II.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Hymn to the Aten</b> is the longest of several hymn-poems addressed to the Aten, the sun-disk worshipped as the supreme god under the 18th Dynasty pharaoh Akhenaten.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li><li><blockquote>The psalm bears a notable resemblance to Akhenaten's <b>Great Hymn to the Aten</b>, written some 400 years earlier in Egypt, and it is often cited as a parallel text.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li></ul></section>
